ationale": "Fifteen polls since early September average roughly nine points for Sand, including recent high-quality surveys from SSRS, Suffolk and Siena, and even Republican-sponsored polls show him ahead. With raters calling the open seat Lean D and markets pricing it near 89%, the forecast sits at Likely D, tempered only by Iowa's Republican baseline.", "watch": "A late debate-driven shift or consolidation of Republican voters behind Lahn could narrow the race, though recent polling shows little movement.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Kim Reynolds (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Iowa's governorship is open this year with Kim Reynolds retiring, and State Auditor Rob Sand faces Republican Zach Lahn in what has become an unusually competitive race for a state that normally leans Republican. Polling since early September consistently shows Sand ahead, with recent surveys from SSRS, Suffolk, Siena and Emerson giving him leads between four and twelve points, and even Republican-sponsored polls showing him in front.\n\nExpert raters broadly call the race Lean D for Democrats, with one rating it Solid D, and prediction markets price Sand's chances near 89 percent. The first debate in early October produced sharp exchanges on taxes, education savings accounts and residency, but polling afterward still showed Sand holding his edge. The forecast expects Sand to win by roughly eight points, with the main uncertainty coming from Iowa's underlying Republican lean and any late movement toward Lahn.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8753, "margin": 5.7}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8859, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8669, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8657, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8472, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.8297, "margin": 5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.8734, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.8592, "margin": 5.5}